description: Repair routing - a broken .NET build or red test suite goes to a resolver seat, not an in-session loop.
globs: "**/*.cs,**/*.csproj,**/*.sln,**/*.slnx,**/*.xaml,**/Directory.Build.props,**/Directory.Packages.props,**/*.targets,**/nuget.config"
alwaysApply: false
---

A broken .NET build or red test suite - default to delegating rather than looping in-session:
fix-the-build goes to **`dotnet-build-error-resolver`** (MC#### errors - WPF XAML markup compile -
are its scope too), make-the-tests-pass goes to **`dotnet-test-failure-resolver`** once the build is
green. The dispatched seat absorbs the repeated build/test output and returns only a diagnosis,
which is the whole point: the red loop's noise stays out of this context.

A resolver that stops as BLOCKED_CONTRACT_CHANGE hit a fix needing a shared-contract change -
outside its bounded scope by design; route it through `project-task-flow`, never edit the contract
to go green.

A seat with no dispatch tool (an implementer or a resolver) does NOT delegate - this routing policy
is the orchestrator's, owned by `project-task-flow`; run your own bounded fix loop and report the red
per your cap. A diagnoser can dispatch, but its one sanctioned dispatch is the evidence-gatherer: it
names the resolver in its report, never dispatches one.

This is guidance, auto-attached by glob (not a hard block).
